    "label": "04 verify with a remote JWKS",
    "disabled": false,
    "info": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "jose_ex4_signkey",
    "type": "jose-key",
    "name": "demo signing JWK (private)",
    "family": "signing",
    "source": "jwk",
    "alg": "auto",
    "secretEncoding": "base64",
    "algorithms": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "jose_ex4_jwkskey",
    "type": "jose-key",
    "name": "demo JWKS URL",
    "family": "signing",
    "source": "remote-jwks",
    "alg": "auto",
    "algorithms": "ES256",
    "url": "http://127.0.0.1:1880/jose-example-jwks",
    "allowInsecureLoopback": true,
    "cacheSeconds": 60,
    "timeoutSeconds": 5
  },
  {
    "id": "jose_ex4_note",
    "type": "comment",
    "z": "jose_ex4_tab",
    "name": "Setup: an EC key pair as JWK, private for signing, public in the served set",
    "info": "This flow publishes a JSON Web Key Set from Node-RED itself and verifies tokens against it over the loopback URL `http://127.0.0.1:1880/jose-example-jwks`. In production the JWKS URL points at your identity provider over https and no loopback opt-in is needed.\n\n1. Generate an EC P-256 key pair locally with Node.js; this writes a private JWK and a public set to new files in the current directory (existing files are not overwritten):\n\n```sh\nnode -e 'const c=require(\"node:crypto\"),f=require(\"node:fs\"); const {privateKey,publicKey}=c.generateKeyPairSync(\"ec\",{namedCurve:\"P-256\"}); const meta={use:\"sig\",alg:\"ES256\",kid:\"example-2026\"}; f.writeFileSync(\"private.jwk\",JSON.stringify({...privateKey.export({format:\"jwk\"}),...meta}),{mode:0o600,flag:\"wx\"}); f.writeFileSync(\"public.jwks\",JSON.stringify({keys:[{...publicKey.export({format:\"jwk\"}),...meta}]}),{flag:\"wx\"});'\n```\n\nKeep `private.jwk` private; only `public.jwks` belongs in the served template.\n2. Open the *demo signing JWK (private)* configuration and paste the **private** JWK into **JWK**.\n3. Open the *serve JWKS* template node and replace the placeholder with `{\"keys\":[<public JWK>]}`.\n4. If Node-RED does not listen on port 1880, edit the *demo JWKS URL* configuration accordingly.\n5. Deploy, then click the inject button.\n\nExpected debug output: the signed token with header `kid: example-2026`, then the verified claims and the protected header in `msg.header`. A token whose `kid` is not in the set leaves on the *rejected* output with `ERR_JWKS_NO_MATCHING_KEY`; a set that cannot be fetched throws `JWKS_FETCH` to a Catch node.",
    "x": 250,
    "y": 40,
    "wires": []
  },
  {
    "id": "jose_ex4_httpin",
    "type": "http in",
    "z": "jose_ex4_tab",
    "name": "GET /jose-example-jwks",
    "url": "/jose-example-jwks",
    "method": "get",
    "upload": false,
    "swaggerDoc": "",
    "x": 170,
    "y": 100,
    "wires": [["jose_ex4_template"]]
  },
